A||N||7||JeffDm||5spd durability with 3800SC engine swap?||||5||
Z||000000||JeffDm||07-26-2001||04:17 AM||||I hate to start a "me too" thread but I couldn't find any specific info in any existing thread.<P>I am trying to gather info on how I can get a 3800 series II supercharged engine to work with a manual and not seriously curtail the life of the transmission.  Is there in production a front drive manual transmission that goes with this engine?<P>I have an '88 with a 2.4 with a 5 speed manual transmission.<P>Who makes the most durable clutch for this?<P>I have read something about three different but mechanically compatible transmissions, Isuzu, Getrag and something else.  How can I identify the transmission?  What is a good way to get the best one for this task?<P>I am told the current prospective engine is from a '97 Riviera.<P>Unfortunately this might be my primary ride and I can't have it disintegrate too quickly for the occasional feel good of acceleration.  This certainly isn't a racing application.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula88||07-26-2001||09:38 AM||||Well, the most reliable way is to use the Automatic tranny that comes with the 3800SC, but that's not what you asked.  Phil has a 3800SC with a 5-speed, and the only thing I've heard him express concern over is the clutch.  Archie has built many V8's with both the Isuzu and Getrag trannies.  If you don't beat on the car, they should last ok.  Same deal with the 3800SC.  Your biggest problem will be getting a good strong clutch to hold the power.  Hopefully Phil, or another 3800SC ower, will post what they're using and the success they've had.  I know West Coast Fiero has a selection of Hi-Perf clutches, but I haven't heard about anyone who's used them.  I've heard lots of good things about West Coast Fiero, but no specific testimonials on the clutches.  I've only heard bad things about the hi-po clutch from the Fiero Shop.  You might consider the Centerforce Dual-Friction clutch Archie sells for his V8's.  If it'll hold a 350's torque, it should be able to handle the 3800SC.||||reg||6
Z||000002||Raydar||07-26-2001||09:41 AM||||You said you have a 2.4? I'm guessing it's a 2.5 if it's a 4 cyl. You have an Isuzu transmission. This is most easily identified by the two cables coming in from above. The Getrag (only 5-speed that came with the V6) has only one cable coming in from above.<BR>One of our list members (Phil) has a 3800 SC through a Getrag and is very happy with it. You may have seen it in Hot Rod, a few months back. On the other hand, several folks have V8s through Isuzus, and have not had any difficulties. <BR>AFAIK, the 3800 was never available with a front-drive manual tranny. <BR>I'm sure others can add to this. <BR>Did you search the Archives? Link is at the top of the page.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steve Normington||07-26-2001||10:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JeffDm:<BR><B>I have read something about three different but mechanically compatible transmissions, Isuzu, Getrag and something else.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>BTW, the something else is the Muncie. This is a 4 spd manual that came with 84 4 cylinders and 85 & 86 V-6's.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JeffDm||07-26-2001||10:34 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>You said you have a 2.4? I'm guessing it's a 2.5 if it's a 4 cyl. You have an Isuzu transmission. This is most easily identified by the two cables coming in from above. The Getrag (only 5-speed that came with the V6) has only one cable coming in from above.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the info!  Yes, it is a 2.5 4cyl.  I messed up in thinking of a 2.4 I saw in some other GM car.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>On the other hand, several folks have V8s through Isuzus, and have not had any difficulties.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'll do some more searches.  I found one page that had cautions, but I must find it again.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>AFAIK, the 3800 was never available with a front-drive manual tranny. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I didn't think so but if someone knew of one I would like to as well.<p>[This message has been edited by JeffDm (edited 07-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||07-27-2001||09:05 PM||||Don't do it! the fiero transmission will NOT handle the torque of an inline 4 Iron Duke that hasn't even been started yet!  It will fail because of gremlins in the engine not liking it much!<P>&lt;/sarcasm&gt;<P>Seriously, go right ahead. There are plenty of people with the 3800SC and the Isuzu 5-speed (the tranny you have) and their cars are working quite well.  <P>I have seen evidence of only ONE fiero transmission that was "blown" by it's engine.<BR>Tina's 383 Stroker hot SCB. 400+ lb-ft.<P>And acording to the pictures, my diagnosis is that the pressure plate first overheated, then *exploded* and that (not too big of an engine) caused the tranny to subsequently fail.<P><BR>If you think you might be making over 400lb-ft of torque, and or over 400bhp (and or over 8,000rpms on the input shaft...) then maybe it is time to think about transmission longevity.  Otherwise, there is simply *no* evidence to the contrary.<P>PS: use Red Line MTL tranny fluid. Extrordinarily Cheap insurance, and your gearbox will simply work better!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000006||Tina||07-28-2001||09:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Don't do it! It will fail because of gremlins in the engine not liking it much!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>You gotta embrace those critters  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Once they figure that you love to tinker, they just disapear.<BR>They just can't stand anybody liking them.<P>Trannywise, go for it.<BR>Clutchwise, went with LUK last year(no complaints)<BR>But switching to Archie's set up due to some added engine mods in the next couple days.<P>Tina  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>BTW just keep in mind,anything will break if you beat on it long enough.<BR>In the muncie tranny case, it was continous burnouts for a about half an hour.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||AOB||07-28-2001||11:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B><BR>I have seen evidence of only ONE fiero transmission that was "blown" by it's engine.<BR>Tina's 383 Stroker hot SCB. 400+ lb-ft.<P>And acording to the pictures, my diagnosis is that the pressure plate first overheated, then *exploded* and that (not too big of an engine) caused the tranny to subsequently fail.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Actually the fiero transmissions are strong enough for Tina's sbc. Last years muncie held up all summer long to Tina daily driving and my occasional (he he) abuse. It wasn't until she decided to park monster for the winter that an abuse test was in order. After a half hour of pavement painting the unit failed. First the input and intermediate shaft went their separate ways causing the transmission case to split down the middle, the clutch was still very much intact. With clutch released everything was smooth, if clutch engaged the car would shake like a dog sh**ing peach pits. The damage to clutch and pressure plate (minus the hot spots) was done racing engine with broken/out of balance input shaft.<P>Eric  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><P>||||reg||1
